  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.read_node.fn` has one obligation. Criterion code uses `ValidationContext::read`, which the text cites as `requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.ValidationContext.read`, to read a node. The text has no MUST, SHOULD or MAY clause of its own beyond that pointer. It adds no conditions, boundaries or failure cases. The read behavior and the recorded fingerprint belong to the cited requirements, which are context here and not reassessed.\n\nThe marked test is `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[direct_reads_return_complete_unicode_contents_and_snapshots]`. It calls `ValidationContext::read` directly on a node and asserts the returned contents equal the original. It checks this for an empty string and for a long multi-byte Unicode string, and it checks that the contents stay unchanged after the underlying graph is modified. It also covers the failure case: reading a missing node returns the error `\"missing\"` and records no dependencies. So it exercises the public `read` entry point named by the requirement and asserts its outcome.\n\nI read the implementation (`crates/trust-but-verify/src/procedure.rs#impl[ValidationContext<'d, D>]#2/fn[read]`). It carries `{impl}` markers for this requirement. It delegates to `read_snapshot` and returns its content. The test is marked with `{test}` for the subject, so there is no marking gap.",
